<owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#MilliArcsecond">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/siacceptedunits#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf>
    <owl:Restriction>
      <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_d088a3cb_d3e3_4eb2_9897_00aef0cb00cd"/>
      <owl:hasValue rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">0.0</owl:hasValue>
    </owl:Restriction>
  </rdfs:subClassOf>
  <rdfs:subClassOf>
    <owl:Restriction>
      <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_d4e0a0ab_2f67_4f87_a0db_b544e6dceed4"/>
      <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#Arcsecond"/>
    </owl:Restriction>
  </rdfs:subClassOf>
  <rdfs:subClassOf>
    <owl:Restriction>
      <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8189b42e_0995_423a_a26c_51168b27c3cf"/>
      <owl:hasValue rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">4.848137e-09</owl:hasValue>
    </owl:Restriction>
  </rdfs:subClassOf>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8246541a_f1f6_4d03_8bd7_fc6b76d17375"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_a3a701ed_6f7d_4a10_9aee_dfa1961fc7b7"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_cdc962d8_f3ea_4764_a57a_c7caa4859179"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">MilliArcsecond</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A minute of arc, arcminute, or minute arc (MOA), is a unit of angular measurement equal to one sixtieth (1/60) of one degree (circle/21,600), or π /10,800 radians. In turn, a second of arc or arcsecond is one sixtieth (1/60) of one minute of arc. Since one degree is defined as one three hundred and sixtieth (1/360) of a rotation, one minute of arc is 1/21,600 of a rotation.  the milliarcsecond, abbreviated mas, is used in astronomy.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
  <core:altLabel xml:lang="en">Milli ArcSecond</core:altLabel>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">MilliArcsecond</core:prefLabel>
  <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/MilliARCSEC</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
  <emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">m''</emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652>
  <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">mas</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
  <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">A minute of arc, arcminute, or minute arc (MOA), is a unit of angular measurement equal to one sixtieth (1/60) of one degree (circle/21,600), or π /10,800 radians. In turn, a second of arc or arcsecond is one sixtieth (1/60) of one minute of arc. Since one degree is defined as one three hundred and sixtieth (1/360) of a rotation, one minute of arc is 1/21,600 of a rotation.  the milliarcsecond, abbreviated mas, is used in astronomy.

-- QUDT</emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
  <emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minute_of_arc</emmo:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
