<owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#CubicInch">
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/units/otherunits#"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf>
    <owl:Restriction>
      <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_d088a3cb_d3e3_4eb2_9897_00aef0cb00cd"/>
      <owl:hasValue rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">0.0</owl:hasValue>
    </owl:Restriction>
  </rdfs:subClassOf>
  <rdfs:subClassOf>
    <owl:Restriction>
      <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8189b42e_0995_423a_a26c_51168b27c3cf"/>
      <owl:hasValue rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">1.638706e-05</owl:hasValue>
    </owl:Restriction>
  </rdfs:subClassOf>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_8246541a_f1f6_4d03_8bd7_fc6b76d17375"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_868ae137_4d25_493e_b270_21ea3d94849e"/>
  <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_9141801c_c539_4c72_b423_8c74ff6b8f05"/>
  <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">CubicInch</rdfs:label>
  <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The cubic inch is a unit of measurement for volume in the Imperial units and United States customary units systems. It is the volume of a cube with each of its three sides being one inch long. The cubic inch and the cubic foot are still used as units of volume in the United States, although the common SI units of volume, the liter, milliliter, and cubic meter, are continually replacing them, especially in manufacturing and high technology. One cubic foot is equal to exactly 1728 cubic inches.

-- QUDT</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
  <core:altLabel xml:lang="en">Cubic Inch</core:altLabel>
  <core:prefLabel xml:lang="en">CubicInch</core:prefLabel>
  <emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/IN3</emmo:EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc>
  <emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">[cin_i]</emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652>
  <emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">[in_i]3</emmo:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652>
  <emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64 rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">in³</emmo:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
  <emmo:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">The cubic inch is a unit of measurement for volume in the Imperial units and United States customary units systems. It is the volume of a cube with each of its three sides being one inch long. The cubic inch and the cubic foot are still used as units of volume in the United States, although the common SI units of volume, the liter, milliliter, and cubic meter, are continually replacing them, especially in manufacturing and high technology. One cubic foot is equal to exactly 1728 cubic inches.

